\section{Bootstrapping without losing a baseline}

Load list helpers and derived operations from Scheme source after installing the primitives. Run the same library tests once in the minimal interpreter and again if a bytecode engine is introduced. Check the fixed test results and record peak pending control frames for an allocation-free tail loop. Also keep a test for shadowing a primitive with a local procedure; the initial environment is not a keyword table. These tests cover separate layers and make failures local enough to diagnose. The dossier's meta-circular compiler discussion (Chapter~\ref{ch:meta-circular}) takes this layering farther: self-hosting is valuable, but it does not remove the need to identify what the original loader and executable supply.
