\section{Saving the First Operand in a Pending Addition Frame}

An evaluator loop is easiest to debug if one can see exactly
where intermediate values live. Implement addition for a small
core with integer literals, binary addition, and an input
primitive \texttt{read-int}. This teaching mode evaluates
operands left to right, a permitted choice when the source
does not fix order. Each addition uses a frame with either a
right-hand expression and its environment or an evaluated
left-hand value. The distinction matters in a complete
evaluator: in the first case the saved environment may contain
roots, and in the second case the saved value may itself
have a heap representation. Given an expression and an
input stream, return the result and the unconsumed stream.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input expression $e$, environment $E$, stream $I$
  \Output integer result and remaining input
  \State $K\gets$ empty stack
  \Loop
    \If{$e$ is $\mathsf{Add}(l,r)$}
      \State push $\mathsf{NeedRight}(r,E)$ onto $K$
      \State $e\gets l$ \Comment{Begin with the left operand}
    \Else
      \State $v\gets\Call{AtomOrRead}{e,I}$
      \While{top of $K$ is $\mathsf{Combine}(u)$}
        \State pop $K$; $v\gets u+v$
      \EndWhile
      \If{$K$ is empty}
        \State \Return $(v,I)$
      \EndIf
      \State pop $\mathsf{NeedRight}(e,E)$ from $K$
      \State push $\mathsf{Combine}(v)$ onto $K$
      \Comment{Keep the left value while evaluating the right}
    \EndIf
  \EndLoop
\end{sudocode}

For \verb|(+ (read-int) (+ 2 (read-int)))| and input
$[5,8,11]$, first push the outer right expression and read
$5$. The outer frame becomes $\mathsf{Combine}(5)$.
Entering the inner addition puts another awaiting frame
above it. The literal $2$ turns that frame into
$\mathsf{Combine}(2)$, and the second read yields $8$.
The loop pops the inner frame to get $10$, then the outer
frame to get $15$. The stream still contains $11$.
Reversing the evaluation order would not change the sum in
this example, so also record the source position associated
with each read during a regression test.

The frames always describe unfinished computations ordered
from innermost down to outermost. A read on an empty stream
reports failure without executing another frame. The loop
uses space proportional to the maximum number of pending
additions, not necessarily the full tree size. In a moving
collector, register all heap-valued fields of those records
as roots and update their slots after relocation; knowing
only the current expression is insufficient.
